MEF File Reduction recipes

F. Selman, Paranal 20 May 2011
(F. Selman, La Silla 17 February 2005)

The recipes found in this package use the originals
written by N. Devillard as starting point.

They have been extensively modified to work with
WFI MEF files directly, both as input and output.
I decided to keep the images allways within a
MEF file which is their logical place, at least
in the context of a primary processing. For example,
the flat field routines now keep track of the
median in each of the extension and save the value
in the respective BSCALE keywords. This save us
from the need to do a gain harmonization of
the chips later on, as the flat field give us
that directly if we take the precaution of
normalizing the flat field frames with a single
number for all the extensions. Thus, the user
shoudl work in ADUs until after the frames have
been flat fielded.

The basic modus operandi is to go extension by 
extension applying the already proven eclipse
routines. 

Version 1.5 of these recipes deal correctly
with the following issues:

- Input and output to MEF files
- Proper treatment of overscan correction,
  subtracting a line rather than a single
  number, as the overscan varies with y.
- Proper treatment of the BSCALE and BZERO
  keywords.
- Proper treatment of the CRVALi, and CRPIXi
  keywords, specially after trimming. The
  WFI headers can be corrected.
- Reading instrument configuration from
PAF file.

These recipes are intended as efficient and
simple to use, voiding the complexities of
a pipeline.

These recipes should allow the users to do the
initial pre-processing of their data correctly.

Version 2.0 should handle correctly the following:

TBD: 
     - Creation of a wfi_ccube version to manipulate
       MEF files.
     - Creation of a wfi_peak version to extract info
       on objects in the images
     - Determination/refinement of WCS and inclution
	in headers


F. Selman	2011-05-20

REAME FROM ORIGINAL VERSION:

The recipes found in this directory have been written in the context of the
BeoWFI project, aimed at providing a data reduction facility for the WFI
instrument based on a Beowulf cluster.

The recipe functionalities are drawn from the eclipse library. The recipe
code has been written by N. Devillard, based on inputs from Aniello Grado,
Andreas Wicenec, Stefan Bogun, Miguel Albrecht, Peter Quinn. The Beowulf
demonstration cluster at ESO is maintained by Dieter Suchar and Andreas
Wicenec.

N. Devillard
Wed Oct 25 13:14:02 CEST 2000

--------------------------------------------------

    Installation
    ------------

	------------------------------------------------------
	Please read the file called INSTALL in this directory.
	------------------------------------------------------

	The installation procedure changes somehow with each distribution,
	it is necessary to read it through to know exactly what has
	changed recently.

    Documentation available with the distribution
    ---------------------------------------------

	o	Manual pages can be found in:
		eclipse/man/man1 in man page format.
		eclipse/man/html1 in HTML format.

	o	A short User's guide in Postscript can be found in:
		eclipse/doc/ps/eug.ps.gz

	o	Lists of Frequently Asked Questions about eclipse and the jitter
		command can be found in:
		eclipse/doc/faq
		You will find two versions of each FAQ list: one in ASCII for
		printout and fast access, and one in HTML for browsing.

    Documentation on the WWW
    ------------------------

    o   eclipse home page at the European Southern Observatory (Germany)
        http://www.eso.org/eclipse

    Mailing-list
    ------------

	The eclipse mailing-list is used to announce new releases, major
	patches, bugs, send ideas about how to use eclipse, etc.
	This list is moderated, only eclipse maintainers are allowed
	to send e-mail to this alias.

	Anyone is free to subscribe/unsubscribe.

    To subscribe: send a mail to <majordomo@eso.org>
    with no subject, the body part should contain only the following
    words:

    subscribe eclipse

    To unsubscribe, send a mail to the same address, with no subject,
    the body part containing only the following words:

    unsubscribe eclipse

	FAQ
	---
	
	A list of frequently asked questions is maintained daily on the Web.
	You can reach it at:
	http://www.eso.org/eclipse/faq

	Bugs
	-----

	You might happen to see the following kind of message:


         \_/   **** fatal error in pid 28123
        '-0-'  **** Segmentation fault or Bus Error
        --0--
        .-0-.
	
	... which means that you are the happy finder of a genuine bug.
	Please report it as soon as possible, indicating the name of
	the executable that produced this, and the context in which the
	command was running.

	HELP US CRUSH BUGS OUT OF ECLIPSE!

    Contacts
    --------
    Bug reports should be sent to <eclipse-team@eso.org>.

